<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Madiun</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Madiun is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, on the Madiun river in the interior of the island. In the late sixteenth century it was the centre of an east Javanese principality that resisted the rise of Mataram and fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panembahan-senapati">Panembahan Senapati</a> around 1590. In 1948 it gave its name to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madiun-affair">Madiun Affair</a>, a communist uprising crushed by the Indonesian Republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/madiun.jpg" alt="Madiun" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anantohermawan1988 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kantor_Pemerintahan_Kota_Madiun_tahun_2020.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Madiun lies in the western part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>, 169 kilometres south-west of Surabaya and 38 kilometres south-east of Ngawi. It stands on the Madiun River, a tributary of the Bengawan Solo River, at an average height of 63 metres above sea level. The city is surrounded by mountains: Mount Wilis, 2,169 metres high, to the east, the Kapur Selatan range to the south and Mount Lawu, 3,285 metres high, to the west. Its climate is a tropical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> one, with moderate to little rain from June to October and heavy rain from November to May.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The best-known episode in the city's modern history is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madiun-affair">Madiun Affair</a> of 1948. After the signing of the Renville Agreement that year, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> units and militias under the influence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-indonesia">Communist Party of Indonesia</a> (PKI) were ordered to disband. In Madiun a group of PKI militia refused to disarm and were killed in September. The killings set off a violent uprising. Army sources claimed that the PKI announced the proclamation of a Soviet Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> on 18 September, with Musso as its president and Amir Sjarifuddin as its prime minister.</p><p>Republican troops suppressed the uprising. On 30 September the Silwangi Division took over Madiun. Thousands of party cadres were killed and 36,000 were imprisoned. Among the executed were several leaders, including Muso, who was killed on 31 October, allegedly while trying to escape from prison. Other PKI leaders, such as D. N. Aidit, went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Madiun has been administratively separate from the surrounding Madiun Regency since the two bodies were formed in 1950, although it remained the seat of the regency administration until 2010. It covers 33.23 square kilometres, is divided into three districts (Manguharjo, Taman and Kartoharjo) and had 195,175 inhabitants at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It is known for its agricultural role and is nicknamed Kota Gadis, an acronym for Trading, Education and Industrial City in Indonesian; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> sometimes called it Milaan van <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. A railway station serves the city, and the Solo-Kertosono Toll Road and Indonesian National Route 20 reach it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Madiun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Senapati subdued Pajang and Demak and campaigned against <b>Madiun</b> and the East Javanese coastal powers led by Surabaya, though he died in 1601 without controlling the north coast.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mataram-sultanate">Mataram Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Dharmawangsa, who had moved the capital to Wwatan near <b>Madiun</b> and married his sister Mahendradatta to Udayana Warmadewa of Bali, invaded Srivijaya by sea in 990 to take Palembang; the Maharaja Sri Cudamani Warmadewa won Chinese support and repelled the invasion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
